// draft
// drafted
type IntersperseError = { code: Text, display: Text }
// drafted
n_error: IntersperseError = { code: "e-n-positive", display: "n must be > 0" }

intersperse(e: Int, iterable: Int[], n: Int) -> Result<Int[], IntersperseError>
	note "put the filler e among the elements of the iterable, leaving n elements between each filler"
	# The output is Ok exactly when n is at least 1, and otherwise it is n_error.

	# The Ok output's first n elements are the iterable's first n elements.

	# If the iterable has more than n elements, the Ok output's element at position n is the filler e.

	# If the iterable has more than n elements, the Ok output from position n plus 1 on is the interspersal of e among the iterable's elements from position n on, with the same n.

	# The iterable's elements appear in the Ok output in the iterable's order.

	# The number of fillers is the Ok output's length minus the iterable's length, and either n times the number of fillers is less than the iterable's length or there are no fillers.

	# The iterable's length is at most n times one more than the number of fillers in the Ok output.

	// example wanted: the docstring's first case, e 0 among [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] with n 1: a filler between every pair
	// example wanted: the docstring's second case, e 0 among [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] with n 2: the last group is one element, and no filler follows it
	// example wanted: an empty iterable with n 1: no element to put a filler between
	// example wanted: an iterable of exactly n elements, [1, 2] with n 2: decided by "more than n", not "at least n"
	// example wanted: an iterable whose length is a multiple of n, [1, 2, 3, 4] with n 2: whether a filler trails the last full group
	// example wanted: e equal to one of the iterable's values, e 2 among [1, 2, 3] with n 1: the fillers are told apart by position, not by value
	// example wanted: n 0 with [1, 2]: the boundary of the error
	// example wanted: n -1 with an empty iterable: the error is decided by n alone, even when there is nothing to intersperse
